\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\newcommand{\Zq}{\ensuremath{\mathbb{Z}_q}}
\newcommand{\concat}[2]{\ensuremath{#1 \,\Vert\, #2}}
\renewcommand{\pair}[2]{\ensuremath{(#1, #2)}}
\newcommand{\triple}[2]{\ensuremath{(#1, #2, #3)}}
\newcommand{\xor}{\ensuremath{\oplus}}
\title{Notes on Authenticated Key Exchange}
\author{R. Tanaka}
\date{May 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Preliminaries}
Let $g$ generate a group of prime order $q$, and write $\Zq$ for the exponent ring.
A participant samples $a \in \Zq$ uniformly and publishes $g^a$.

\section{Handshake}
The initiator sends $\concat{pk_A}{pk_B}$ together with a fresh nonce.
Both parties later confirm the full triple $\triple{g^a}{g^b}{g^{ab}}$ before deriving keys.
The session key is $k = \hkdf{g^{ab}}{\mathrm{nonce}}$ and the confirmation tag is $t = k \xor c$.

\section{Discussion}
The pair $\pair{pk_A}{pk_B}$ binds the transcript to both identities,
so a man in the middle cannot rebind the nonce to a different peer.
\end{document}

